<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dutty Boukman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1767–<a href="/years/1791">1791</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Boukman Dutty, Zamba Boukman, Boukman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dutty Boukman was a leader of the Haitian Revolution, born to a Muslim family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegambia">Senegambia</a> in about 1767 and enslaved first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>. Sold for trying to teach other enslaved people to read, he became a slave driver and later a coach driver, which let him travel between plantations, carry news and encourage resistance. According to some contemporary accounts, he and the priestess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecile-fatiman">Cécile Fatiman</a> presided over the ceremony at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bois-caiman">Bois Caïman</a> in August 1791, and on 22 August he led the rising at the Noé <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> refinery, usually taken as the start of the revolt. He was killed by French soldiers in November 1791 defending a rebel base.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Haitian folklore holds that it was sparked by a Vodou ritual at Bois-Caïman in 1791 and that the early revolutionaries <b>Boukman</b> and François Mackandal were oungans, though historians debate these claims.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Their freedom of movement made some of them leaders of resistance: Jean-Jacques Dessalines rose from the cane fields to be a driver, and <b>Dutty Boukman</b>, a driver and later a coachman, helped lead the rising of 1791.</blockquote><a href="/societies/saint-domingue">Social Hierarchy of Saint-Domingue</a></li></ul></section>
